§9"It's all right", I said.     "She's all right."

"I brought her back,             it's okay now."

And our friends unscrewed their faces and made the usual sorts of sounds
#- §9- thank goodness you were there, they said, how lucky she was that you were there.

And I didn't say: §oPlease. As if I would leave anything to luck.
§0
§9We went home. After she was settled, I slipped the Book back in its hiding place, and the other things too:
#- §9The bronze and copper knife (so hard to make), the bone rattle, the fatty candle shaped like a child's hand. 

The amulet that looked like a different set 
of knots from 
every angle.

The much too smooth green stone.
#- §9I kept the cards. 
The cards are useful even without the Book.

She is still my love, my seven-days-wonder. She smiles just as often (though at different things) as she did before. 
Her eyes are still deep and hooded, with that merry, secret twinkle in them. 
#- §9Her needs, I explain, will be different now. 

But not so difficult to manage. §oNot so very.
§0
§9After all, we have so §omany §0§9friends.

And time... 

Oh, we will have 
            §oso much time.
